Inconsistent Initialization Identified.

VULNERABILITY NAME or TYPE
Inconsistent Initialization

'''
DETAILED DESCRIPTION
The `CompositeMultiOracle` contract contains an inconsistent initialization of the `updateTime` accumulator in both the `peek` and `get` functions.

In `peek` and `get`, the local/return variable `updateTime` is implicitly initialized to `0` (the default for `uint256`). This variable is then passed as `updateTimeIn` to the internal `_peek` / `_get` functions. Inside `_peek` / `_get`, the aggregated update time is computed as:

```solidity
updateTimeOut = (updateTimeOut < updateTimeIn) ? updateTimeOut : updateTimeIn; // Take the oldest update time
```

Because `updateTimeIn` starts at `0`, the condition `updateTimeOut < updateTimeIn` is always false for any non-negative `updateTimeOut`. As a result, `updateTimeOut` is always set to `0`. This value then propagates through every iteration of the path and through the final oracle lookup. Consequently, both `peek` and `get` always return `updateTime = 0`, regardless of the actual update timestamps provided by the underlying oracle sources.

This is a security-relevant initialization flaw: integrators may rely on `updateTime` to enforce staleness checks. Since the oracle always reports `0`, consumers could incorrectly treat prices as fresh (or stale), potentially accepting outdated prices or causing denial-of-service conditions in dependent contracts.

The correct behaviour is to aggregate the *oldest* update time across all oracle calls in the path. To do this, the accumulator must start at the maximum possible value so that the first real update time becomes the new minimum.

RECOMMENDED MITIGATION / FIX
Initialize `updateTime` to `type(uint256).max` at the beginning of both `peek` and `get`, before entering the path loop.

For example, in `peek`:

```solidity
function peek(bytes32 base, bytes32 quote, uint256 amount)
    external view virtual override
    returns (uint256 value, uint256 updateTime)
{
    uint256 price = 1e18;
    bytes6 base_ = base.b6();
    bytes6 quote_ = quote.b6();
    bytes6[] memory path = paths[base_][quote_];
    updateTime = type(uint256).max;   // <-- add this line
    for (uint256 p = 0; p < path.length; p++) {
        (price, updateTime) = _peek(base_, path[p], price, updateTime);
        base_ = path[p];
    }
    (price, updateTime) = _peek(base_, quote_, price, updateTime);
    value = price * amount / 1e18;
}
```

Apply the same initialization in the `get` function:

```solidity
updateTime = type(uint256).max;
```

With this change, the first `_peek` / `_get` call will set `updateTime` to the source’s actual update time, and each subsequent call will correctly retain the minimum (oldest) update time along the entire composite path.
'''
Detector: InconsistentInitializationDetector
Classification: logic (no standard SWC)
